As a reminder to myself, platformIO build commands:

From the PlatformIO project folder—the one containing platformio.ini—run:

`pio run -t upload`

If PlatformIO doesn’t automatically select the correct serial port:

`pio run -t upload --upload-port COM5`

## Installing the firmware

Ready-built firmware is on the GitHub
[Releases](https://github.com/astechevolves/storon/releases) page; no
PlatformIO needed.

- **First install** on a blank ESP32-S3 (N16R8): flash
  `storon-vX.Y.Z-full.bin` at offset `0x0`, in the browser with
  [ESP Web Flasher](https://espressif.github.io/esptool-js/) (Chrome or Edge),
  or with `esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 storon-vX.Y.Z-full.bin`.
- **Updating** a running StorOn: upload `storon-vX.Y.Z-update.bin` on the
  Settings page under **Firmware update**. Settings and spool links are kept.

Releases are built by GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/firmware-release.yml`)
when a version tag is pushed: `git tag v0.4.0 && git push origin v0.4.0`.

## Setup and web UI

The board is configured from a web page it serves itself; no code edits are needed.

1. Flash the firmware (`pio run -t upload`).
2. On first boot with no WiFi saved (or if the saved WiFi can't be reached), the
   board starts a setup hotspot named `StorOn-Setup-XXXX`, password `storonsetup`.
   Join it with a phone or laptop and the settings page opens (or browse to
   http://192.168.4.1/settings).
3. Enter WiFi, MQTT broker, pins, and enable the RFID lanes that have a reader
   (six lane slots, each with its own SS pin), then **Save and reboot**.
4. Afterwards the UI is at http://filament-monitor.local/ (the device ID with
   `_` changed to `-`) or the board's IP address.

Pages:

- **Status** (`/`): WiFi/MQTT state, sensor readings, fan, each RFID lane
  (reader found, current tag, hit counts) and an event log. **Read tag**
  dumps a lane's tag: UID, type, SAK and any memory readable without keys.
  NTAG stickers read fully; Bambu Lab (MIFARE Classic) tags show the UID and
  locked sectors, because their data is encrypted.
- **Settings** (`/settings`): everything above, an optional admin password
  (user `admin`), firmware upload (`.pio/build/esp32-s3-devkitc-1/firmware.bin`),
  reboot and factory reset.

Holding the BOOT button for 10 seconds erases all settings (factory reset).

Copying `include/config.example.h` to `include/config.h` pre-fills WiFi and MQTT
at build time, so a fresh board connects without the setup hotspot.

JSON API: `GET /api/status`, `GET /api/lane/dump?lane=N`, `GET|POST /api/settings`.

## RFID layout: one reader per lane

Each lane has its own RC522 reader, with a ferrite sheet behind it so it only
reads its own spool. Spools always load the same way round, so each spool is
identified by the single tag facing its reader.

**Not done in this iteration: shared readers.** Fewer readers could cover the
same lanes by mounting one in every other gap between spools, each reading the
tag on the spool to its left and the spool to its right. That needs:

- Firmware that reads two tags per reader (read one, halt it, read the next)
  and reports per reader instead of per lane.
- A way to tell which of the two tags belongs to which lane. Either learn
  each tag's side once (a spool's right-side tag means it's in the reader's
  left lane), or match a newly seen tag to the lane whose filament just moved
  (the LLL-Plus buffers report feed motion to the Pi, not to the ESP32).

## Spool tags

Each lane decodes the tag on the spool in front of it and publishes the result
as attributes on its Home Assistant sensor (`<device_id>/lane_N/attributes`):

- **Bambu Lab spools** (MIFARE Classic 1K): read-only. Keys are derived from
  the tag UID and the data decoded per the Bambu Research Group's published
  format: material, color, weight, temperatures, and `tray_uuid`, the same ID
  the AMS reports and OpenSpoolMan stores in Spoolman's `tag` field.
- **OpenTag3D stickers** (NTAG215/216, opentag3d.info spec 2.004): read and
  write. On the Status page, **Write tag** opens a form for a lane; with a
  Spoolman URL set in Settings it can pre-fill from a spool number and save
  the sticker's ID back to that spool's `tag` extra field (create a Text extra
  field with key `tag` in Spoolman first). Writes only touch user memory and
  are verified by reading back.

Remaining filament stays in Spoolman; OpenTag3D has no live remaining field.

## Branding assets

The logo and icons served by the web UI live in `assets/`. After changing
them, regenerate the C header with `python assets/make_web_assets.py`.
